{
  "dom_id": "step-q109542",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find, in point-slope form, the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-1,4)$ and $(5,2).$",
  "worked_solution": "We know that the line passes through the point $(-1,4),$ so we can use $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,4)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{2-4}{5-(-1)}=\\frac{-2}{6}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,4)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
